          "text": "filename: docs/knife-sharpening.md\n---\n# How to Sharpen a Kitchen Knife by Hand\n\n## Gather Your Tools  \nBefore you begin, gather the necessary tools to ensure a safe and effective sharpening process. You will need a high-quality whetstone—preferably a natural or synthetic stone with a medium to fine grit, such as 800 or 1000 grit. A sharpening rod or a bench block provides stability and helps maintain the correct angle. A towel or cloth is essential to protect your work surface and to wipe the knife after sharpening. Additionally, have a pair of scissors or a knife block nearby to safely store your knife once sharpened. A magnifying glass or a small flashlight can help you inspect the edge for imperfections. Make sure your knife is clean and dry before starting. A sharp knife is safer and more effective in the kitchen, so proper maintenance is key.\n\n## Sharpen the Edge  \nHold the knife at a consistent angle—typically between 15 and 20 degrees—using your dominant hand. This angle is crucial for achieving a clean, durable edge. Place the beveled edge of the knife against the whetstone and move it in a smooth, forward motion, applying even pressure. Use a back-and-forth motion, starting from the heel of the blade and moving toward the tip. Repeat this motion several times on each side of the blade to ensure even sharpening. Always maintain the same angle throughout the process to avoid dulling or damaging the blade. After a few strokes, switch to a finer grit whetstone if needed to refine the edge. This step may take 10 to 20 minutes depending on the knife’s condition and the sharpness you desire.\n\n## Test and Store  \nAfter sharpening, test the knife’s edge by gently drawing it across a piece of paper or a thin sheet of aluminum foil. The blade should glide smoothly without resistance. If it still feels dull, repeat the sharpening process with more strokes or a finer whetstone. Once satisfied, wipe the blade with a clean towel to remove any dust or debris. Store the knife in a knife block or a sheath to protect the edge and prevent damage. Always keep the knife dry and avoid exposing it to moisture or heat. Regular sharpening not only improves performance but also extends the life of your kitchen tools. A well-maintained knife is a chef’s best friend—both in function and in safety. Remember, the right angle and consistent use of a whetstone are essential for long-lasting results.",
